WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
- We are back and I'm hanging
with Justin Timberlake.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:03.911
Give it up, everybody!

00:00:03.911 --> 00:00:04.903
(audience cheers)

00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:06.670
One of my favorite things about Justin

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:10.050
and seeing him live is that
he loves a horn section.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:10.890
I do as well.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.320
Instruments like the sax,
trumpet, and trombone

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.110
definitely breathe a
special energy into music.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.870
Our next guest is a very
talented trumpeter who came -

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.759
I don't say "trumpeter"
enough, it's a weird word -

00:00:20.759 --> 00:00:23.400
who came to a stark realization when he

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.233
was growing up.

00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:26.542
He didn't know any female trumpeters.

00:00:26.542 --> 00:00:28.950
Now he's working to change all of that

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.900
with his New Orleans-based nonprofit,

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.460
Girls Play Trumpets Too.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.204
Please welcome Troy, everybody!

00:00:34.204 --> 00:00:35.596
(audience cheers)

00:00:35.596 --> 00:00:37.198
Meet Justin.

00:00:37.198 --> 00:00:38.820
(audience claps)

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.470
So you said growing up,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.660
you didn't see any females
playing trumpet, right?

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.400
- Right, so my first
instrument was a violin.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.780
I played that for a couple years

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.453
and then I picked up the
trumpet in fourth grade,

00:00:47.453 --> 00:00:49.920
so growing up in New Orleans, you know,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.040
it's a brass city, drum city-

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:52.873
- Musical city.

00:00:52.873 --> 00:00:53.706
- -but I didn't see-

00:00:53.706 --> 00:00:55.170
it's a musical city,
the birthplace of jazz,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.480
and so I didn't see
any professional female

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:58.770
trumpet players growing up,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:00.660
so I felt like girls and women could not

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.520
play the trumpet on
the professional level.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.420
- Absolutely.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.760
Whenever I heard you say that earlier,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.461
I was like, "I don't
know that I did, either,"

00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:08.730
now that I'm thinking about it.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.440
Even like I love a horn section on stage,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:11.910
and I don't know that I noticed-

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.820
you have brass, always
involved in every show you do.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.990
- Yes, the energy just gets lifted

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:18.990
with brass accompanying.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.100
- [Kelly] It's sexier.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:20.933
- Yeah.

00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:22.740
- Yeah, I like a trumpet.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.300
Well, you have a theory, though,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.340
of why few women play, right?

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.430
- Well, yes, because the
trumpet's a lead instrument

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:31.260
and it represents leadership,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.870
and so for a long time,
especially band directors

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.960
would discourage girls from
playing that lead instrument

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.850
'cause they felt like
they were not leaders

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:39.840
and they were not strong enough,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.310
so they would put them
on a woman instrument

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.143
like a flute.

00:01:42.143 --> 00:01:43.590
"You go play that flute!

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.390
You're not strong enough
to play that trumpet."

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.370
And so growing up in New
Orleans, like I said,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.050
I didn't see any professional
female trumpet players

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.120
until I saw one that came-

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.060
she came from Canada to New Orleans

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.476
and performed at Dillard University

00:01:56.476 --> 00:01:59.760
and she was playing a lot
of Miles Davis's tunes.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.590
And Miles Davis is one of
my favorite trumpet players,

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.207
besides Louis Armstrong, you know?

00:02:03.207 --> 00:02:05.580
And so I was like, "Wow, she can play!"

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.810
So that knocked down the walls of

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:08.490
gender bias within me and then traveling

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:10.200
around the world seeing professional

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.510
female trumpet players that I actually

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.650
became their best friend, you know,

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:14.760
like, okay, yeah.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.050
- Yeah, it's inspiring.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.340
So, who would you say your-

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.900
I think my favorite female musician

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:19.733
would probably be Sheila E.

00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:21.480
I just love working with
her and I love her so much

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.810
and I love percussion 'cause
I can't keep time like that.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.270
But who's one of your
favorite female musicians?

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:28.533
- Oh gosh, so many.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:30.840
Nina Simone.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:31.770
- [Kelly] Oh, yeah.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.012
- That's my favorite, too.

00:02:33.012 --> 00:02:35.340
(crosstalk)

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.810
- Yeah, I mean, Etta.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:37.784
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:37.784 --> 00:02:42.180
- Because for me, you
know, I think sometimes

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:43.447
somebody like Whitney
or Etta would say, like,

00:02:43.447 --> 00:02:44.880
"Oh, they're your favorite singers,"

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.490
but they were musicians, you know,

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.930
to the full extent.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.190
And, I mean, there's so many-

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.360
I could keep going on and on,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.620
but we only have so much show.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.697
- [Kelly] No, Aretha, as well, yeah.

00:02:53.697 --> 00:02:54.585
- Aretha, I was about to say-

00:02:54.585 --> 00:02:55.740
(crosstalk)

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:56.860
- Everybody knows the
singer, but great pianist.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.320
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.120
How did you start Girls Play Trumpets Too,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.250
like where did that kind of start?

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:03.990
- So, you know the devastation of

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.149
how Hurricane Katrina
devastated New Orleans.

00:03:06.149 --> 00:03:09.490
And, you know, traveling around the world

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.030
and being away from home and seeing all my

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:19.020
fellow just New Orleanians
being devastated

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.310
by that storm.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.800
And I got away, you know, I evacuated.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.450
So I felt like it was my responsibility

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.700
to come back and go back to New Orleans

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.890
to help rebuild New Orleans, yeah,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.650
and then I got inspired
to join this organization

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.110
that would get professional
musicians that have degrees

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.653
to teach at a school
that didn't have enough

00:03:35.653 --> 00:03:38.100
money in their budget
to pay for a full-time

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.140
music teacher, and that
organization's called

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:40.973
Artist Corps New Orleans,

00:03:40.973 --> 00:03:42.600
so it got me into actually teaching music

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.610
in the school system and I'm the only

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.410
black male teacher there, so I feel like

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.790
they were looking at me and I feel like

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.680
they needed me and I needed them.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.300
And I said, "You know
what, imma keep it going."

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:55.350
And I decided to put a after
school program together,

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:57.150
specifically focusing on the trumpet,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.130
teaching everyone how to play the trumpet,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.340
but my best trumpet player was a girl.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.173
- Yeah.

00:04:03.173 --> 00:04:05.910
- And she said, "Do you
know who Valaida Snow is?"

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:06.840
I said, "No, I don't."

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.820
She's like, "You don't
know who Valaida Snow is?

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.440
What's wrong with you?"

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.090
- So disappointed in you.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:12.923
- She was, she was.

00:04:12.923 --> 00:04:15.360
So Valaida Snow was a black woman

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.650
that played the trumpet that traveled

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.780
all over the world in 1920s,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.030
and so she's our inspirational model

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.470
for Girls Play Trumpets Too.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.517
I was scared at first of like,

00:04:24.517 --> 00:04:26.250
"I don't know if I can do this program,"

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.510
but my lady encouraged me to do it.

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:28.650
She said, "That's it."

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.560
And I was trying to come
up with different names

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.393
for the program.

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:33.780
She's like, "No, Girls Play Trumpets Too,

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:34.890
T-O-O, that's it.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:36.030
Keep it simple."

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:36.863
Yes, so.

00:04:36.863 --> 00:04:37.696
- It's very memorable.

00:04:37.696 --> 00:04:38.790
She's got good ideas.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.500
- Every time I trumpet, I win.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.333
- Every time I trumpet, I win.

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:42.918
There you go!

00:04:42.918 --> 00:04:44.687
(audience cheers)

00:04:44.687 --> 00:04:45.958
Nice callback!

00:04:45.958 --> 00:04:49.458
(upbeat jazz music plays)

00:04:54.894 --> 00:04:57.342
(audience cheers)

00:04:57.342 --> 00:04:58.302
What's up, everybody?

00:04:58.302 --> 00:05:01.303
I'm back with Justin Timberlake and Troy!

00:05:01.303 --> 00:05:03.247
Troy is the founder of
Girls Play Trumpets Too

00:05:03.247 --> 00:05:05.940
in New Orleans and you
just saw a couple of

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:07.740
his students showing off their skills,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:11.370
so Troy's nonprofit offers
girls more than music lessons.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:12.990
It's also creating a sisterhood,

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.190
which is very important.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.050
Joining us now are two of his students,

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.020
9-year-old Bailey and
11-year-old Crissana.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:19.853
Give it up!

00:05:19.853 --> 00:05:21.888
(crowd cheers)

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:23.221
Welcome to the show, ladies.

00:05:23.221 --> 00:05:25.443
Meet Justin, I know you know Troy.

00:05:25.443 --> 00:05:26.690
(Bailey giggles)

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:27.523
So I have to ask.

00:05:27.523 --> 00:05:30.360
So you were amongst the
first to join, right?

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.320
So what was it like?

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.730
What was the first experience?

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.190
- Well, when I joined
Girls Play Trumpets Too,

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:37.620
I was very nervous because I couldn't even

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.010
play not one note, like at all.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.530
- [Kelly] Yeah, you gotta start somewhere.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.720
- I couldn't even hold the trumpet.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:44.670
But now I can actually play all the notes.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.320
- Okay, nicely done.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.850
What about you, Bailey?

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.190
- It was okay because, you know,

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.550
it was like really
inspiring because, you know,

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.971
you get a little nervous sometimes,

00:05:55.971 --> 00:05:57.900
but at the same time, you know,

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.362
like Crissana said, we could
not play a note at all.

00:06:01.362 --> 00:06:03.180
(audience and Justin laugh)

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.447
- Those are when the parents are like,

00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:06.049
"Mhm, just keep going."

00:06:06.049 --> 00:06:07.020
(Crissana laughs)

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:09.090
I played violin when
I was a kid, and well,

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.139
there's a reason why I still don't.

00:06:11.139 --> 00:06:12.240
(Crissana laughs)

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.800
Yeah, so it was not good.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.450
But what do you think you've
learned about yourselves?

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:16.590
'Cause it's also about connecting.

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:17.700
Like when I was in choir as a kid,

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:19.920
I loved also the friendships that I made

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.890
in the class, so it's
about that, as well, right?

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.660
- I learned that trumpet
actually brings out

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.780
a lot of joy in me.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:28.770
- Yeah.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.603
It's a joyous sound.

00:06:29.603 --> 00:06:30.909
That's so beautiful and simple!

00:06:30.909 --> 00:06:31.983
(audience claps)

00:06:31.983 --> 00:06:32.940
I love that.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.683
What about you, Bailey?

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.040
- It's the same thing like Crissana said.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:40.560
It also brings out the
frequency, you know?

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.970
Like all the music in one whole bunch.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:47.580
It really brings out so
much joy in everybody.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:48.690
- You know what you just said?

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:50.820
I love that, like the feeling of it.

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:53.250
'Cause I remember I sat in
on a session in a studio

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:54.930
and it was like a string section, horns,

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.250
there's a whole orchestra was there,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.541
and sitting in the room,

00:06:57.541 --> 00:07:01.020
you feel like the vibrations,
the wind from the instrument.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:02.880
It's a magical thing to feel.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.276
I'm glad you brought that up.

00:07:04.276 --> 00:07:06.510
And to be in tune with
that at such a young age

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:07.650
is so cool.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:08.790
What do you see in these girls?

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.740
Like these qualities in these girls?

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.590
- So one thing about Crissana,
I've been teaching her

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.510
for a couple of years, and she will always

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.030
bring this joy and I called her Smiley,

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.460
and she's always smiling, see,
you look at her right now.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:21.690
I called her Smiley, then when she

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.340
joined Girls Play Trumpets Too,

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:25.200
she said, "Oh, my stage name is CoCo,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.033
call me CoCo."

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:27.412
So, welcome to the stage, CoCo, everyone!

00:07:27.412 --> 00:07:28.245
- [Kelly] CoCo!

00:07:28.245 --> 00:07:29.498
(audience cheers)

00:07:29.498 --> 00:07:31.060
You got a stage name?

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.750
I like it.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.550
- The one thing I can say about Bailey,

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.590
she's a deep thinker.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.540
Her responses are deep,

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.530
and she will come early
to Girls Play Trumpets Too

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.787
before all the girls
come because she's like,

00:07:45.787 --> 00:07:48.090
"I hope they don't come so
I can get a private lesson."

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:48.923
(audience laughs)

00:07:48.923 --> 00:07:50.940
So that says a lot about her

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.980
and I call her the Serena Williams-

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.390
- [Kelly] That's a good work ethic.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.223
- Yeah.

00:07:55.223 --> 00:07:56.760
You gotta put that extra
time in and, you know,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.010
she really wants to get some extra time

00:07:59.010 --> 00:07:59.970
to get better.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.080
- I love that.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.000
It takes dedication to
get good at something,

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.110
so that's awesome.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.183
Do you have any advice for the girls?

00:08:07.126 --> 00:08:09.009
- I feel like I want advice from them.

00:08:09.009 --> 00:08:10.860
(Kelly and Troy)

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.720
I remember being so young and being

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:15.750
so inspired by music and knowing that

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.640
it affected me in such a positive way,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.720
so just to hear how you're
so tapped into like-

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.690
it really is a different
frequency, you know?

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.600
And also, too, the relationships

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:31.680
that you will make in your
life with other musicians

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.890
that share this same feeling will be

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:37.080
relationships that you will never forget.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.910
They will become your extended family,

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.037
like I talked about
musicians that tour with me

00:08:41.037 --> 00:08:42.600
and the musicians that I get to play with

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.480
in the studio and so many
friendships that are made

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.930
over this feeling that you share about

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.750
creating music together and I don't even

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:52.583
know what to say.

00:08:52.583 --> 00:08:53.512
I'm so blown away by these two-

00:08:53.512 --> 00:08:54.345
- It's a blessing to be a part of.

00:08:54.345 --> 00:08:57.030
- -these two young old souls over here.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.200
- Yeah.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.450
So where do you see the
program going from here?

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:01.590
- So we wanna expand.

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:04.140
We're actually expanding
two schools for us,

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.550
creating partnerships with schools

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.320
to do after school programming

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.270
and so we're actually in
need of more trumpets,

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:15.000
more music stands, more method books,

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.400
and funds to actually pay the instructors

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.950
to teach at these different schools.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:21.450
- Especially 'cause
they're taking music and

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:23.490
art programs out of schools, so like the

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.080
after school programs are so important.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.400
- Right, so important.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.350
And I wanna expand it to the world

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.840
to where we can actually
do it online, and so-

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.370
- Yeah, and reach everybody.

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:33.510
- Reach everyone.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.390
Because girls and women
can play trumpet, too.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:37.320
- Yeah.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.313
Yeah, you can.

00:09:38.313 --> 00:09:39.245
(audience cheers)

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:42.800
Well, there is a surprise that Justin has,

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:45.490
so I'll let you take over from here.

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:49.260
- In my hometown in
Memphis, I'm very hugely

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.810
involved with Stax Academy,
which is the music program

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:56.810
for, you know, young people in Memphis

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:59.463
and I remember being-

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.190
I think it was probably
third or fourth grade

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.950
